Multifunctional gold nanocluster decorated metal-organic framework for real-time monitoring of targeted drug delivery and quantitative evaluation of cellular therapeutic
## response
Key Laboratory of Analytical Chemistry for Biology and Medicine (Ministry of Education), Department of Chemistry, Wuhan University, Wuhan 430072, China
Therapeutic drug monitoring is central to optimize therapeutic efficacy and minimize adverse events, however, the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of most drugs differ greatly among individuals. Evaluation of the therapeutic response is in urgent need to help clinician predict the clinical dose of drug. Herein, we described an analytical assay using gold nanoclusters (AuNCs) decorated metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for targeted drug delivery and monitoring pharmacodynamics, giving attractive options for studying individualized therapy. The camptothecin (Cam) anticancer drug was caged in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) MOFs. Modification with pegylated folate (FA) for specifically recognizing folate receptor on cancer cell membrane significantly improved the delivery efficiency. Intercellular delivery of Cam initiated programmed death of cells and upregulated the apoptosis indicator (caspase-3), which cleaved the peptide linker between AuNCs and MOFs. The quenched fluorescence of AuNCs was then recovered once the peptide was enzymatically cleaved by caspase-3. Real time monitoring of targeted drug delivery was achieved by imaging of the light-up fluorescence in HepG2 cells, while the amount of caspase-3 could be quantified by detecting Au in the released AuNCs in inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) with a limit of detection (LOD) of \(0.12\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). Our assay emphasizes the application of multifunctional nanomaterial for therapeutic self-monitoring and quantitative evaluation of cellular drug uptake and therapeutic response, allowing the acceleration of drug evaluation.
Keywords: Cancer therapy; multifunctional nanoprobe; caspase-3; fluorescence imaging; inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry; dual-mode therapeutic self-monitoring
## Introduction
Cancer is a major threaten to public health worldwide, and millions of deaths and new cases occur every year even with the rapid evolution of modern medicine and the continuous improvement of medical conditions\(^{1,2}\). Apart from the most direct way of surgical resection and the widely employed methods of radiotherapy or chemotherapy in clinic, some new cancer treatment strategies including chemotherapy, photodynamic therapy (PDT) and photothermal therapy (PTT) are extensively studied and reported\(^{3}\). In these therapeutic systems, a series of molecules such as antibodies\(^{4}\), peptides\(^{5}\), aptamers\(^{6}\) and some small molecules\(^{7}\) are employed in the targeted delivery of anti-cancer drugs to weaken side effects, and various nano-vehicles involving liposomes\(^{8}\), proteins\(^{5}\), dendrimers\(^{9}\), metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)\(^{10}\) and carbon materials\(^{11}\) are developed as drug carriers to enhance local therapeutic concentration and release drugs in a well-designed manner. These systems promise far more effective cancer treatment, but lack the ability of monitoring drug release and therapeutic process in real time through some specific indicators such as the levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) or enzymatic activity, which can help understand the therapeutic mechanism and evaluate the therapeutic efficiency.
To address these issues, some strategies have been proposed to perform self-monitoring imaging during cancer therapy in a portion of works\(^{12-14}\). For example, Ju and coworkers\(^{14}\) built a drug carrier system based on graphene oxide, which was conjugated with pegylated folate (FA) for active targeting to cancer cells and fluorescein-labeled caspase-3-specific substrate peptide for caspase-3-based fluorescence self-monitoring of the therapeutic process. Additionally, chemo-photodynamic dual-therapy and cathepsin B-activated cancer cell imaging were integrated into a multifunctional MOF-based nanoprobe by Lei and coworkers in a similar way\(^{12}\). MOFs are an emerging kind of crystalline hybrid porous material with characteristics of large specific surface area, adjustable properties, biodegradability, abundant modifiable sites and functional group diversity. In addition, they have been proven to be an excellent quencher for fluorophore based on the mechanisms of fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET), photoinduced electron transfer (PET) or charge transfer\(^{15}\). These outstanding advantages render
MOFs, especially nano-scaled MOFs, ideal substrates to be multi-functionalized with multifarious molecules, which have been introduced to the field of bioimaging and drug nanocarrier systems\(^{16}\).
Admittedly, real-time fluorescence imaging of these indicators in living cells can reflect their spatiotemporal variation and evaluate the therapeutic efficiency, but the auto-fluorescence of complex biological matrix and the sensitivity of fluorescence intensity to environmental variation make only rough qualitative results available in most cases, hindering their accurate quantification which can reveal cell states more precisely and offer a deeper insight into therapeutic mechanism. The need and challenge for accurate quantification of therapeutic self-monitoring indicators calls for further development of cancer therapy systems.
Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS), a powerful detector for element-specific analysis, shows extraordinary quantitative ability with advantages of high sensitivity, wide linear range, strong resistance to matrix and multi-element analysis ability. An increasing number of ICP-MS-based methods with element labels have been reported for the quantification of small organic molecules\(^{17}\), proteins\(^{18,19}\), DNA\(^{20}\), RNA\(^{21}\), cells\(^{22,23}\), bacteria\(^{24}\) and viruses\(^{25}\), demonstrating its superiority in bioanalysis; while ICP-MS cannot provide in-situ noninvasive imaging information. In our previous research work\(^{22,26}\), some efforts have been made to remedy this defect by ingeniously integrating ICP-MS quantification and fluorescence imaging into a single nanoprobe which employs metal-containing fluorescence nanoparticles like quantum dots and upconversion nanoparticles or utilizes the excellent quenching ability of nanoparticles like gold nanoparticles. Gold nanoclusters (AuNCs) are made up of several to several hundreds of gold atoms and exhibit fluorescence with high quantum yield and good stability\(^{27}\), which can also be used as dual-function nanoprobe for both ICP-MS quantification and fluorescence imaging. In addition, this design can be extended to therapeutic self-monitoring of multifunctional drug-carrier systems by simultaneous qualitative and quantitative analysis of therapeutic monitoring indicators in living cells during cancer therapy.
Herein, a multifunctional MOF-based nanoprobe was developed for both tumor-targeted drug delivery and dual-mode therapeutic self-monitoring (Scheme 1). First of all, as an excellent drug nano-vehicle and quencher, nano-scaled NH\(_2\)-MIL-101(Fe) was loaded with a
substantial amount of camptothecin (Cam) via noncovalent encapsulation. With FA as a guiding unit, the nanoprobe would be taken up selectively by FA receptor high-expressed cancer cells (HepG2 cell as model cells) through endocytosis mediated by receptor-ligand interactions, and the encapsulated anti-cancer drugs would be released from the nanoprobe in HepG2 cells. Subsequently, the released drugs initiated programmed death of cells and caspase-3 was activated as a main participant of cell apoptosis. To monitor the treatment process, AuNCs were conjugated on to the MOFs through a peptide, which is the substrate of the apoptosis indicator (caspase-3)\(^{28}\). Once the peptide was enzymatically cleaved by caspase-3, the quenched fluorescence of AuNCs on MOFs was then recovered. The lighten-up fluorescence allows in-situ and real-time imaging of caspase-3. The amount of caspase-3 could be quantified by detecting Au in the released AuNCs by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS). This work provides a robust multifunctional platform to realize targeted chemotherapy and dual-mode therapeutic evaluation by both fluorescence imaging and ICP-MS quantification, making it a desired assay for evaluating therapeutic process or studying apoptosis process induced by some specific factors like drugs\(^{29}\) or pollutants\(^{30}\).
## Experimental section
Apparatus and materials. A 7500a ICP-MS (Agilent Technologies, Japan) equipped with a Babington nebulizer was used for the determination of \({}^{197}\mathrm{Au}\) and the operating parameters for ICP-MS detection are listed in Table S1. An UltraVIEW VoX living cell confocal imaging system (PerkinElmer, USA) was employed in fluorescence imaging of cells. Other instruments are detailed in Supporting Information (Text S1).
Caspase-3 substrate peptide with an amino acid sequence of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-CCYKKKDEVDDDDCOOH (DEVD, purity: \(95\%\) ) and the control group peptide of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-CCYKKKDEVGDDDDCOOH (DEVG, purity: \(95\%\) ) were obtained from Sangon Biotechnology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Recombinant caspase-3 was purchased from BioVision Company (Milpitas, CA, USA). Caspase-3 inhibitor Ac-DEVD-CHO was acquired from Beyotime Biotechnology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), glutathione (GSH) and FA-PEG-COOH (M.W. 5000) were purchased from Aladdin Reagent Inc. (Shanghai, China). Cam was obtained from Macklin Inc. (Shanghai, China). Other reagents used in this work was illustrated in Supporting Information
(Text S1).
Preparation of AuNC/Cam@MOF. Nano-scaled \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) (MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)) and Cam@MOF was synthesized by the method reported in previous work\(^{31}\). Detailed steps are depicted in Text S2. For synthesis of peptide-AuNCs, some modifications were made on the preparation process reported in previous work\(^{32}\). Detailed steps are depicted in Text S3.
AuNC@MOF was prepared as follows. Firstly, \(50\,\mathrm{mg}\) of EDC·HCl, \(100\,\mathrm{mg}\) of sulfo-NHS and \(0.5\,\mathrm{mg}\) of FA-PEG-COOH were introduced to \(9\,\mathrm{mL}\) of freshly prepared peptide-AuNCs solution, and the mixture was shaken at room temperature in the dark for \(40\,\mathrm{min}\) (mixture A). After that, \(10\,\mathrm{mg}\) of MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was dissolved in \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) of HEPES buffer solution (\(10\,\mathrm{mM}\), pH 7.2), and the obtained solution of MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was added to mixture A. The mixed solution was kept shaking at room temperature in the dark for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\), followed by centrifuging at \(12{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\), and the precipitate was washed twice with ultrapure water to gain purified MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) modified with peptide-AuNCs and FA-PEG-COOH. The final products of AuNC@MOF were re-suspended in water and stored in dark in \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) refrigerator for further use.
AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe was synthesized by similar methods as described above except for replacing MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) by Cam@MOF.
Fluorescence analysis and ICP-MS quantification of exogenous caspase-3. A \(1\times\mathrm{PBS}\) containing AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe \((500\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and caspase-3 of different concentrations was incubated under \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2.5\,\mathrm{h}\) with a shaking speed at \(200\,\mathrm{rpm}\). Subsequently, to acquire the fluorescence emission spectra of the released AuNCs in the presence of different concentrations of caspase-3, each mixed solution was directly measured by synchronous scanning fluorescence spectrometry at room temperature \((\Delta\lambda=74\,\mathrm{nm}\), scanning range: \(550{-}800\,\mathrm{nm}\), slit width for excitation: \(10\,\mathrm{nm}\), slit width for emission: \(10\,\mathrm{nm}\)). To acquire accurate quantification results of caspase-3, the mixed solution was centrifuged at \(12{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) to sedimentate the unreacted AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe, and the released AuNCs in the supernatant was introduced to ICP-MS for monitoring \({}^{197}\mathrm{Au}\).
Inhibitor dose-response assay for caspase-3. Caspase-3 (\(10\,\mathrm{ng\,mL^{-1}}\)) was incubated with its
inhibitor (Ac-DEVD-CHO) of different concentrations for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\), and the solutions were shaken under \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2.5\,\mathrm{h}\) after the introduction of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe \((20\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\). After centrifugation for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) at \(12{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm}\), \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of supernatants were introduced to ICP-MS for monitoring \({}^{197}\mathrm{Au}\).
Fluorescence imaging and ICP-MS quantification of activated caspase-3 in HepG2 cells. HepG2 cells in logarithmic growth phase (detailed cell culture procedure is illustrated in Text S4) were dissociated and seeded to confocal dishes with \(1\!\times\!10^{5}\) cells each, followed by maintaining under \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(5\%\,\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). Afterwards, AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe \((20\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) in fresh medium was spiked into the cells, followed by incubating for another \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) under the same conditions. The cells were washed with \(1\!\times\!\mathrm{PBS}\) and then the activated caspase-3 in HepG2 cells was imaged by laser scanning confocal microscope using \(561\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser as excitation wavelength.
As for ICP-MS quantification, the incubation process was the same as fluorescence imaging except that the cells were seeded to 6-well plates with \(1\!\times\!10^{6}\) cells per well. After incubation, the culture medium was replaced by \(1\!\times\!\mathrm{PBS}\) and the cells were scraped off the 6-well plates by a cell scraper. The detached cells were lysed by ultrasonic in ice water for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\), followed by centrifugation at \(12{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) under \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the supernatants were subjected to ICP-MS detection of \({}^{197}\mathrm{Au}\), and external standard method was employed to quantify activated caspase-3 in HepG2 cells through the signal intensities of Au.
Three control groups were constructed for both fluorescence imaging and ICP-MS quantification. One group involved AuNC@MOF nanoprobe instead of AuNC/Cam@MOF, one group was subjected to an extra addition of inhibitor \((10\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) for caspase-3, and the other group involved DEVG peptide-AuNCs in place of DEVD peptide-AuNCs for the construction of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe, respectively.
MTT assay. Cell viability was investigated by MTT assay in this work. Detailed steps are depicted in Text S5.
Therapeutic self-monitoring. HepG2 cells \((1\!\times\!10^{5})\) were seeded into each confocal dish and
maintained under \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\). After the addition of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe \((20\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1})\), the cells were incubated for 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 h, respectively, and then washed by PBS. The therapeutic self-monitoring was performed by laser confocal imaging of these dishes with an excitation wavelength of \(561\;\mathrm{nm}\).
## Results and discussion
Structure characterization and functionalization. \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) was the initial matrix to prepare AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe, and the synthesized nano-scaled \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) showed an octahedral morphology with particle size of about \(100{-}200\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 1A left). The mean hydrodynamic diameter of the synthesized MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is appr. \(153\ \mathrm{nm}\), as given by dynamic light scattering in Figure 1B. Compared with the super-resolution SEM of the nanoprobe before peptide-AuNCs modification (Figure 1A left), the prepared AuNC/Cam@MOF (Figure 1A right) showed the similar octahedral morphology and size distribution. However, obvious white spots appeared on the surface of the nanoprobe after modification, which was attributed to the grafted AuNCs. The good dispersibility of the nanoprobe also contributed to intracellular assays.
The FT-IR spectrum of MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Figure S1) validated the existence of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC ligand structure in its framework by showing the characteristic absorption of benzene ring (1545, 1496 and \(1421\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) and the stretching vibration absorption of O-H and N-H bonds (3431 and 3378 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)), C-H bond (2961 and \(2925\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)), \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) bond (1679 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)), C-O bond (1254 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) and C-N bond (1157 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)). Figure 1C displayed the XRD pattern of self-prepared MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) power, which was consistent to that reported by previous work\(^{33}\).
In the fluorescence spectra (Figure 1D), peptide-AuNCs showed a maximum excitation wavelength of \(588\ \mathrm{nm}\) and a maximum emission wavelength of \(662\ \mathrm{nm}\) that closely matched with the AuNCs prepared by template of similar peptides\(^{34}\). MALDI-TOF-MS characterization of the peptide-AuNCs (Figure S2) showed six intense peaks, with the spacing between adjacent peaks equal to a peptide of 1576 Da. It is consistent with the formula of \(\mathrm{Au}_{14}\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{m}}\mathrm{S}_{12-2\mathrm{m}}\) (where P is CCYKKKDEVDDDD, \(\mathrm{m}=0{-}6\); S is sulfur atom), suggesting that the peptide-AuNCs are made up of 14 gold atoms and 6 peptides, benefiting the fluorescence imaging. The assembly of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe was validated by several techniques. First
of all, the stretching vibration absorption at \(1040\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of C-S bond from cysteine appeared only in the FT-IR spectrum of AuNC/Cam@MOF rather than MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Figure S1). The absorption of AuNC/Cam@MOF in the range of \(200{-}250\ \mathrm{nm}\) was much stronger than MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Figure S3), which was in accordance with the absorption characteristics of peptide-AuNCs. Furthermore, the zeta potentials of MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and Cam@MOF changed from positive states (\(15.0\;\mathrm{mV}\) and \(17.2\;\mathrm{mV}\)) into negative states (\(-41.8\;\mathrm{mV}\) and \(-36.5\;\mathrm{mV}\)) after the conjugation of peptide-AuNCs and FA-PEG-COOH (Figure 1E). These results all indicated that the AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe was prepared successfully.
The number of the peptides labeled on each nanoprobe was estimated with the details illustrated in Text S6 in Supporting information. The results indicate that there are ca. 356 AuNCs corresponding to 2136 peptides labeled on one nanoprobe.
The stability of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe was investigated by adding the nanoprobe into PBS for \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\), and the signal intensity of Au in the supernatant was obtained by ICP-MS and compared with total Au on the nanoprobe added in PBS. The result of Au in the supernatant was less than \(1\%\) of that obtained by introducing the probe into ICP-MS after acid digestion (Figure S4), indicating a good stability of the prepared AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe.
Optimization of experimental conditions. To ensure the excess of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe to caspase-3, the effect of the concentration of AuNC/Cam@MOF (\(1{-}40\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) on the signal intensity of Au detected by ICP-MS was investigated with the concentration of caspase-3 fixed at \(10\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}}\). As illustrated in Figure 2A, the signal response of Au increased when the concentration of nanoprobe was increased from 1 to \(20\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) and then reached a plateau after \(20\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). It indicated that \(20\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\) nanoprobe was sufficient to be hydrolyzed by \(10\;\mathrm{ng}\) caspase-3, and the mass ratio of nanoprobe to caspase-3 was kept above 2000 in the following experiment.
To obtain stable signal response and shorten analytical time as well, we studied the influence of incubation time on the signal intensity of Au in the range of \(0.2{\sim}4\;\mathrm{h}\) (Figure 2B). The Au signal settled out after \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\) incubation, so \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\) was chosen as the incubation time for the following experiments.
Inhibitor dose-response curve for caspase-3. To confirm that the Au signal was generated by the hydrolysis of caspase-3 substrate peptides, the intensity change of Au was investigated by adding caspase-3 inhibitor of different concentrations before the addition of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe in the caspase-3 solutions. As shown in Figure 2C, the Au intensity decayed with increasing the concentration of inhibitor, proving that the generation of Au signal was indeed derived from the cleavage of substrate peptides by caspase-3.
Selectivity of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe. AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe was supposed to image and quantify caspase-3 in cells, but the intracellular matrix is so complex that we validated the selectivity of this nanoprobe by selecting some common inorganic ions, small molecules, peptides, proteins and proteases in cells as interfering substances. The results are exhibited in Figure 2D, from which we can see that obvious signal response of Au only existed in the group containing caspase-3 and the other groups showed similar responses to the blank group. It indicated that the nanoprobe had an excellent selectivity and showed strong resistance to common inorganic ions, small molecules and macromolecules in organisms.
What's more, the effects of potential cell matrix on the detection of caspase-3 was investigated. Cell lysate was obtained from HepG2 cells and used as the cellular matrix; the response of caspase-3 in terms of Au signal without and with the presence of the cellular matrix was compared. The group without the presence of the cellular matrix was set as the control group. From the results shown in Figure S5, the signal of Au has no obvious difference between the control group and the groups in the presence of the cellular matrix at three levels (cell lysate from \(5 \times 10^{5}\), \(1 \times 10^{6}\), \(1.5 \times 10^{6}\) HepG2 cells, respectively). It indicates that the intracellular matrix has little effect on the detection of caspase-3 in the proposed system.
Analytical performance. Under the optimal conditions, the analytical performance of this work based on AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe for ICP-MS quantification of caspase-3 was investigated, and the dependence of Au signal on the concentration of caspase-3 is exhibited in Figure 3. The linear correlation equation was \(I_{\mathrm{Au}} = 64825c_{\mathrm{caspase-3}} + 170919\) (R²=0.9925) with a good linear range of 0.3-150 ng mL⁻¹ and a limit of detection (LOD, 3σ) of 0.12 ng mL⁻¹, and the relative standard deviation (RSD) was 6.5% for seven replicate quantifications of 1.0 ng
mL\(^{-1}\) caspase-3. Table S2 lists the comparison of this work with some other reported works\(^{35-47}\) in terms of LOD for caspase-3. The LOD of our work is comparable to the other works using different kinds of tags or detectors\(^{35-47}\). It should be mentioned that most of works\(^{35-46}\) are not designed to detect intracellular caspase-3. Compared to the work\(^{47}\) which is also designed to detect intracellular caspase-3 by ICP-MS, the multifunction platform of our work can realize the targeted delivery of anti-cancer drugs and real-time monitoring of cellular therapeutic response based on caspase-3 as the biomarker.
Fluorescence response of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe to exogenous caspase-3. In the presence of caspase-3, peptides from peptide-AuNCs conjugated on AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe can be specifically hydrolyzed due to their amino acid sequence of DEVD, and then the fluorescence of AuNCs quenched by MOF-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) will be recovered. To validate this "off-on" process, the fluorescence responses of AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe to caspase-3 of different concentrations were recorded. As depicted in Figure S6, the fluorescence enhanced constantly with the rising concentration of caspase-3. It demonstrated that the prepared AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe was able to detect caspase-3 based on the mechanism of PET inhibition, which could be further applied to the fluorescence imaging of intracellular caspase-3.
Cell viability after therapeutic treatment. To evaluate the potential of the constructed AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe in tumor targeted chemotherapy, MTT assays of HepG2 cells incubated with the nanoprobe for different time were performed. Because Cam loaded on the nanoprobe acts as a chemotherapeutic agent and the surface-modified FA acts as a guiding molecule to cancer cells, MTT assays were also performed for the cells incubated with AuNC@MOF without loading of Cam and FA-free AuNC/Cam@MOF, respectively. As shown in Figure S7, the viability of HepG2 cells were still above 80% after 24 h incubation with AuNC@MOF, and dropped rapidly over time with the presence of AuNC/Cam@MOF (only 20% left after 24 h incubation). It demonstrated that AuNC@MOF nanoprobe had low cytotoxicity and good biocompatibility, and it started to play the anti-cancer role only after loading with Cam. Moreover, the cell viability obtained by AuNC/Cam@MOF incubation declined faster than that obtained by FA-free AuNC/Cam@MOF incubation; it validated the
contribution of FA molecules modified on the surface of AuNC/Cam@MOF to the targeted delivery of Cam into cancer cells.
Fluorescence imaging of activated caspase-3 in HepG2 cells. After proving that the AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe possessed excellent imaging and quantitative capabilities for exogenous caspase-3 with high selectivity and stability, we further applied them to living cell imaging and ICP-MS quantification of endogenous caspase-3 activated by cell apoptosis during chemotherapy. A series of confocal imaging assays were performed to evaluate the therapeutic efficiency and imaging possibility of the nanoprobe, and the results are illustrated in Figure 4. Bright red fluorescence was only observed for folic receptor (FR)-positive HepG2 cells incubated with AuNC/Cam@MOF, weak fluorescence was shown for HepG2 cells incubated with AuNC@MOF and FR-negative A549 cells incubated with AuNC/Cam@MOF, and no visible fluorescence signals were acquired for cells in other groups. These phenomena verified the feasibility for AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe to be employed in the analysis of endogenous caspase-3 in living cells based on their "off-on" fluorescence and high selectivity to FA-positive cells.
ICP-MS quantification of activated caspase-3 in HepG2 cells. Fluorescence imaging can only provide qualitative results of intracellular caspase-3. In order to obtain accurate quantification results of intracellular caspase-3, ICP-MS was employed to detect the released peptide-AuNCs, which were separated from AuNC/Cam@MOF by centrifugation according to different particle size. An external standard method was adopted for the quantification by establishing a calibration curve with exogenous caspase-3. The final quantification results obtained by ICP-MS under different conditions are listed in Table 1. A marked increase in caspase-3 content was obtained for cells incubated with AuNC/Cam@MOF than AuNC@MOF, indicating that AuNC@MOF did induce cell apoptosis after loaded with Cam. Moreover, caspase-3 contents were both below the LOD when its inhibitor was added or when DEVD was replaced by DEVG. It testified that the AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe possessed excellent selectivity and specificity to caspase-3 and ICP-MS was well suited for the accurate quantification of activated caspase-3 during tumor chemotherapy. This work provides a simple
technique to screen inducers and inhibitors for caspase-3 family, and offers a convenient and feasible method to study therapy mechanism for diseases such as cancer, autoimmune disease and heart disease.
Therapeutic self-monitoring. It takes a process for anti-cancer drugs from entering cells to releasing to induce cell apoptosis, so we expected that the AuNC/Cam@MOF nanoprobe can achieve self-monitoring of the therapeutic process while targeting anti-cancer drugs to cancer cells. Time course of fluorescence responses for HepG2 cells incubated with AuNC/Cam@MOF was studied (Figure 5), and the detectable reddish fluorescence spots began to appear after \(4~\mathrm{h}\), reflecting that the nanoprobe could enter cancer cells rapidly and then released the loaded drugs, which triggered the apoptosis process and activated caspase-3 in cells. Furthermore, with the extension of incubation time, the fluorescence intensity increased gradually and then stabilized after \(10\,\mathrm{h}\), validating the capacity of the nanoprobe to realize real-time therapeutic self-monitoring during chemotherapy process.
## Conclusion
In this work, we constructed a novel multifunctional nanoprobe of AuNC/Cam@MOF for the targeted delivery of anti-cancer drugs, and dual-mode therapeutic self-monitoring by real-time fluorescence imaging and quantitative ICP-MS detection of activated caspase-3 during chemotherapy. In this nanoprobe, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) MOFs serve as both drug nano-vehicle and quencher of AuNCs, while AuNCs serve as dual-mode tag for fluorescence imaging and ICP-MS quantification, presenting multiple advantages, such as synthetic convenience, good selectivity, high sensitivity, good biocompatibility and strong resistance to complex biological matrix. Complementing real-time monitoring and quantitative detection provides a new approach to study therapeutic mechanism of cancer by integrating chemotherapeutic drug delivery and self-evaluation of therapeutic efficiency. In addition, the design of this multifunctional nanoprobe can be extended to other therapy systems or simultaneous detection and quantitative analysis of multiple intracellular markers.
